  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "A rectangle has integer side lengths.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If 3 is added to the height and 4 is added to the width, then the area is tripled. Find the possible dimensions of the rectangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(h,w)=(2,20),(3,8),(6,4)$, where $h$ is the height and $w$ is the width.\n\nExpand the area equation and check which positive integer heights give integer widths.",
          "worked": "Let $h$ and $w$ be the original height and width.\n$$\\begin{align*}(h+3)(w+4)&=3hw\\\\hw+4h+3w+12&=3hw\\\\4h+12&=2hw-3w\\\\4h+12&=w(2h-3)\\\\w&=\\frac{4h+12}{2h-3}.\\end{align*}$$\nPositive integer widths require $h\\ge2$. The width decreases as $h$ increases and is always greater than 2, so an integer width must be at least 3.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4h+12}{2h-3}&\\ge3\\\\4h+12&\\ge6h-9\\\\h&\\le\\frac{21}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nChecking integer heights $2\\le h\\le10$ gives integer widths only for $h=2,3,6$. Hence the possible dimensions are $(h,w)=(2,20),(3,8),(6,4)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 110
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If 3 is added to the height and 2 is added to the width, then the area is doubled. Find the possible dimensions of the rectangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(h,w)=(4,14),(5,8),(6,6),(7,5),(9,4),(15,3)$, where $h$ is the height and $w$ is the width.\n\nExpand the area equation and check which positive integer heights give integer widths.",
          "worked": "Let $h$ and $w$ be the original height and width.\n$$\\begin{align*}(h+3)(w+2)&=2hw\\\\hw+2h+3w+6&=2hw\\\\2h+6&=hw-3w\\\\2h+6&=w(h-3)\\\\w&=\\frac{2h+6}{h-3}.\\end{align*}$$\nPositive integer widths require $h\\ge4$. The width decreases as $h$ increases and is always greater than 2, so an integer width must be at least 3.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2h+6}{h-3}&\\ge3\\\\2h+6&\\ge3h-9\\\\h&\\le15.\\end{align*}$$\nChecking integer heights $4\\le h\\le15$ gives integer widths only for $h=4,5,6,7,9,15$. Hence the possible dimensions are $(h,w)=(4,14),(5,8),(6,6),(7,5),(9,4),(15,3)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 110
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If 4 is added to the height and 2 is taken from the width, then the area is doubled. Find the possible dimensions of the rectangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(h,w)=(2,6),(3,14)$, where $h$ is the height and $w$ is the width.\n\nExpand the area equation and check the positive integer heights below 4.",
          "worked": "Let $h$ and $w$ be the original height and width, with $w>2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(h+4)(w-2)&=2hw\\\\hw-2h+4w-8&=2hw\\\\4w-hw&=2h+8\\\\w(4-h)&=2h+8\\\\w&=\\frac{2h+8}{4-h}.\\end{align*}$$\nA positive width requires $h<4$, so check $h=1,2,3$. These give $w=\\frac{10}{3},6,14$, respectively. Only the last two widths are integers. Hence the possible dimensions are $(h,w)=(2,6),(3,14)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
